WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.125
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to get Cooking in Cloud,

00:00:02.125 --> 00:00:05.760
where we share the best recipes
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.720
I'm Priyanka, and
in this episode,

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:10.020
we'll share the recipe
to plan a warm DR

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.620
pattern for your application
that are deployed on premise.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.886
So let's dive in.

00:00:15.886 --> 00:00:19.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.160
A little recap.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.430
In the last episode, we were
talking about Main Street art

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.650
that runs through
applications on premise

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.950
and our building a DR
infrastructure on Google Cloud.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.650
And we helped them
set up a cold DR plan.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.950
Well, Main Street art has
changed a bit since then.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.520
They now need to setup a
warm stand by DR pattern

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.180
to keep the RTO and
RPO values as small as

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.840
possible without the effort
and expense of a fully

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.800
HA configuration, which
means to support a disaster,

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.590
they need some
replication and redundancy

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.240
built into the system.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.320
Well, let's see how
this would work.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.300
We create a VPC network, then
we configure the connectivity

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.720
between the on-premise network
and the Google Cloud network.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.990
We replicate the on-premise
servers to the Compute Engine

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:17.010
instances, then create snapshots
of the web and application

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.870
server instances.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:22.020
We then create a custom
image of the database server

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:26.650
on Google Cloud with the same
configuration as on premise.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:30.600
Then we start a database
instance in Google Cloud using

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.080
that custom image and
attach a persistent disk

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.760
to the instance for database
and transaction logs,

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:40.950
configure the replication
of the database server,

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.890
set auto delete flag
on the persistent disk

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.780
to no auto delete.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.810
We do this so that our
persistent disk will not

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:53.430
be inadvertently deleted since
that would be a disaster,

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.620
configure a scheduled
task to create

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.070
regular snapshots of the
persistent disk of the database

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:03.740
instance on Google Cloud, then
test the process of creating

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:06.995
instances from the snapshots
by creating the web

00:02:06.995 --> 00:02:11.510
and application server, also
create a script that copies

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:15.410
updates to the web application
and the application server

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:19.160
whenever the corresponding
on-premise servers are updated,

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:22.100
write the script to create
a snapshot of the updated

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:26.000
service, then configure
Cloud DNS to point

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.680
to the internet-facing
web service on premise.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:31.590
Then a disaster hits.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:35.700
All Main Street art needs to
do is to resize the database

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.940
server instance to handle
the production load,

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.480
use the web and application
server snapshots to create

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.090
new web and application
server instances

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:48.960
and then test that it all works.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:53.580
Once tested, point the Cloud
DNS to their web service

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:54.540
on Google Cloud.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.630
When the production environment
is running on premise again

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.110
and can support
production workloads,

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:03.210
Main Street art has to
reverse the steps that they

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.490
followed during the failover.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.490
A typical sequence to return
to the production environment

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:13.230
is we take a backup of the
database running on Google

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:17.280
Cloud, then copy and apply the
backup files to the database

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.330
in the production environment.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:21.780
Then we prevent connections
to the application

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.130
in Google Cloud.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:26.220
One way to do this is to prevent
connections to the web server

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.770
by modifying the firewall rules.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.740
From this point, the
application will be unavailable

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:34.920
until we finish restoring
the production environment.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.100
Don't forget to copy any
transaction log files over

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.470
to the production
environment and apply

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:43.040
to the database in
that environment.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.630
Test that the application
works as expected

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:50.120
by simulating the user scenarios
on the production environment.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:54.110
Configure Cloud DNS to point
to the on-premise web service,

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:56.870
then delete the web
server and the application

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:00.540
server instances that are
running in Google Cloud,

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.620
resends the database
server on Google Cloud back

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:05.330
to the minimum
instance size that

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:08.420
can accept replicated data
from the on-premise production

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:11.450
database, then
configure replication

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.970
between on-premise
database server

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:16.250
and the database
server in Google Cloud

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:19.399
by following the instructions
for your specific database

00:04:19.399 --> 00:04:20.630
software.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:22.200
Well, there you have it.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.950
If you are running your
application on premise and need

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:28.760
to keep the RTO and RPO
values as small as possible

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.210
without the expense of a
fully HA configuration,

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:35.220
then a warm DR pattern
is the way to go.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.220
We learned how to
approach recovering

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:41.000
the environment from failure
in a warm DR scenario.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.250
That's all for today on
Get Cooking in Cloud.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:46.460
I'm hoping you're cooking
up your own DR strategy.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:48.800
Join us next time where
we will share the recipe

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.900
to implement a hot DR pattern.

